 ### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
 - Since: 2026-09-11
+- Status: SUPERSEDED 2026-10-23 by §check-fails-closed (below), at the maintainer's explicit direction.
 - Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
 - Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
 - Reason: with `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and blocked every commit in
   the repository for a day (2026-09-10). The check is advisory by design.
+
+### §check-fails-closed — an internal failure of the check aborts it with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any failing command aborts the check non-zero so breakage is noticed. Finding large
+  files is still only a warning (exit 0).
+- Reason: the maintainer judged the fail-open behavior hid real breakage. Known risk, from the 2026-09-10 outage: an
+  internal error (e.g. `wc` on a file deleted mid-run) now blocks commits if this runs as a pre-commit hook.
+- Note: under `-e` + `pipefail`, avoid `cmd && echo` as the last statement of a loop body; use `if`.
